.Dd October 1, 2026
.Dt DMESG 1
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm dmesg
.Nd print the kernel's log
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm
.Op Fl krtTwx
.Op Fl l Ar level Ns Op , Ns Ar level ...
.Sh DESCRIPTION
The
.Nm
command prints the kernel's log,
.Pa /dev/bootlog ,
which the kernel fills from its start, one record a line, as Linux's
.Nm
prints its ring buffer.
Each record is a level, the seconds since boot, and the text; by default
.Nm
prints the time and the text, as
.Ql [6.34] net: eth0: IPv4 192.168.0.106/24 .
Root may also read the log with any program that reads a file.
.Pp
The options are Linux's that read the log:
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Fl k , Fl Fl kernel
Print the kernel's records, which are all the log holds.
.It Fl l Ar list , Fl Fl level Ns = Ns Ar list
Print the records of the levels in the comma-separated
.Ar list
only:
.Cm emerg ,
.Cm alert ,
.Cm crit ,
.Cm err ,
.Cm warn ,
.Cm notice ,
.Cm info
and
.Cm debug ,
in either case, or their numbers, 0 to 7.
A level with
.Ql +
after it stands for it and every level of higher priority, and one with
.Ql +
before it for it and every level of lower priority:
.Cm err+
is
.Cm emerg
to
.Cm err .
.It Fl r , Fl Fl raw
Print each record as the log holds it, its level in angle brackets first.
It is taken with
.Fl l
alone.
.It Fl t , Fl Fl notime
Print no time.
.It Fl T , Fl Fl ctime
Print the time of day each record was made, from the boot time in
.Pa /proc/stat ,
in place of the seconds since boot.
.It Fl w , Fl Fl follow
Print the records there are, then each new one as it is made, until
interrupted.
The log is read again each second, and a new record is told by the last one
printed, or by its time once the log's ring has dropped that one.
.It Fl x , Fl Fl decode
Print each record's facility and level before it, as
.Ql kern  :info  : .
.El
.Pp
Short options may be combined, as in
.Fl Tw .
Linux's options that clear the buffer, resize it or set the console's
level are refused, since the log here is not a buffer they could change.
The BusyBox
.Nm
applet is not built, because it cannot read this kernel's log.
.Sh FILES
.Bl -tag -width /dev/bootlog
.It Pa /bin/dmesg
The script, installed on os-real, metal, GNU and recovery images.
.It Pa /dev/bootlog
The kernel's log.
.El
.Sh EXIT STATUS
.Nm
exits with status 1 for an option it does not take, a level it does not
know, or a log it cannot read, and otherwise with the status of the program
that formats the log.
.Sh EXAMPLES
.Dl dmesg -T -l err,warn
.Dl dmesg -w
.Sh DIAGNOSTICS
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Ql dmesg: -c: the boot log, /dev/bootlog, is not cleared, resized or given a console level
An option that would change the buffer was given.
.It Ql dmesg: unknown level 'LEVEL'
A level of
.Fl l
is not one of the eight names or numbers.
.It Ql dmesg: -r is taken with -l alone
.Fl r
was given with
.Fl t ,
.Fl T
or
.Fl x .
.It Ql dmesg: /dev/bootlog: cannot read the log
The caller may not read the log, which is root's.
.El
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr bootlog 4 ,
.Xr rach2 7
